  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-0b32b1dff06c5610": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-237",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "This building must have been of great size to have gathered on its flat roof three thousand people. The blind Samson probably “made sport” on one side of the enclosed courtyard, where the spectators on the roof and the crowds within could see him at the same time. In Algiers, on…"
      },
      "c-25a4c201ea3d6cf4": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-236",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Dagon was the national god of the Philistines. The name is derived from dag, a fish. Dagon is the diminutive of dag, and signifies “little fish”; not so much, however, in reference to size, as to the affection entertained for it; so that some would render it, “dear little fish.”…"
      },
      "c-6a28fe9af876c861": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-235",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Grinding a hand mill was the lowest kind of slave labor. Among the Greeks and Romans slaves were sometimes compelled to do this as a punishment. It was doubtless considered equally degrading in the days of Samson, and for this reason the Philistines condemned him to it after they…"
      },
      "c-c6699dc0500d17d8": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-238",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The two “middle pillars” here spoken of constituted the key of the entire building: these falling, the house would he destroyed. Pliny mentions two large theaters built of wood, and planned with such ingenuity that each of them depended on one hinge. Dr. Thomson suggests, from hi…"
      }
    },
